AB  - This paper proposes a dynamic governance technology for power knowledge warehouse based on multi-system data collaboration within a microservice architecture. This method constructs a power knowledge warehouse representation model using power entities, business relationships, operational events, temporal states, and knowledge versions as unified objects. Data access services, semantic parsing services, knowledge construction services, conflict detection services, dynamic governance services, and query reasoning services are designed to achieve loosely coupled access and continuous governance of cross-system data. To address the risks of cross-system attribute conflicts, state drift, and version overwrite, a conflict resolution algorithm based on source credibility, time freshness, field stability, and historical hit rate is further proposed, and a consistency optimization objective function for incremental updates is established. Experimental results show that, in scenarios with continuous access to multi-source power data, the proposed method outperforms centralized ETL, rule-based governance, and ordinary graph database methods in terms of data consistency, knowledge update latency, and conflict resolution accuracy. The research results demonstrate that combining microservice architecture with knowledge graph governance mechanisms can effectively improve the dynamic maintenance capabilities and cross-system collaboration efficiency of the power knowledge repository.
