AB  - Along with the digitalization of healthcare, massive volumes of unstructured clinical data have been generated. This poses a challenge for clinicians to efficiently retrieve relevant information. In contrast, strict privacy and data sovereignty are considered as the main constraints when using Large language Models (LLMs) in medical environments. This paper proposes DocQA-MS, a privacy-preserving microservices architecture for medical document question-answering. Operating fully offline, the proposed solution employs a local Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) powered by Llama 3.1 to prevent leakage of sensitive personal patient data. A robust multi-stage de-identification pipeline is proposed. It incorporates pattern matching with transformer-based Named Entity Recognition. This system achieves a micro-averaged F1-score of 94.7% and an AUC of 0.978 in detecting Protected Health Information (PHI). Experimentation prove the accuracy and context-awareness of the delivered responses with compliant performance latencies. The proposed system thus provides a good solution for clinical decision support, rigorously respecting the privacy standards.
ER  - 

