AB  - To address heterogeneous data integration, low-value use of unstructured documents, and poor knowledge retrieval in Xinjiang Oilfield, this study develops a specialized knowledge-sharing platform based on microservices and a knowledge graph. The research process involves three phases. Phase one builds up the exploration and production domain-knowledge ontology based on the Exploration and Production Data Model (EPDM), covering the whole business process from seismic data, logging data, mud-logging data, and overall research process. Phase two puts forwards a hybrid process of knowledge processing based on the Apache Tika engine's capacity to process multiple-document formats and by combining the CRF algorithm with rule dictionaries from different domains in terms of automatic entity identifications and semantic annotations in the geological domain. Phase three realizes key functions like semantic-enhanced queries, map-aid navigation queries, and intelligent dynamic energy data through the combination of Elasticsearch's full-text search function and WebGIS. The system has been deployed in Xinjiang Oilfield's private cloud network and has effectively regulated more than 50,000 retired research texts from the Mahu project research team and the archives center. Real-world testing proves that more than 200 simultaneous accesses can be supported in a relatively high concurrency level in a short span of 3 seconds. The system provides effective solutions in overcoming the "information silo-effect" caused by research data, leading to substantial improvements in know-down reuse efficiency and providing research ideas and methods towards digital transformation in oilfield enterprises.
